<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

People read times, distances, and temperatures in the units they grew up with, and expect calendars to start on
the day their week starts. Script can format numbers with <code>Intl</code>, but a static page, a page with script
blocked, or a component that renders on the server before script runs can't.

These features let a page include both forms and show the one the user reads:

<div class=example id=example-units>
    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;p>Opens at &lt;span class=h12>9:00 AM&lt;/span>&lt;span class=h23>09:00&lt;/span>.
       &lt;span class=fahrenheit>72°F&lt;/span>&lt;span class=celsius>22°C&lt;/span>,
       &lt;span class=miles>3 miles&lt;/span>&lt;span class=km>5 km&lt;/span> from the station.&lt;/p>
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=css>
    .h23, .celsius, .km { display: none; }
    @media (hour-cycle: h23) { .h12 { display: none; } .h23 { display: inline; } }
    @media (temperature-unit: celsius) { .fahrenheit { display: none; } .celsius { display: inline; } }
    @media (measurement-system: metric) { .miles { display: none; } .km { display: inline; } }
    </pre>
</div>

<div class=example id=example-calendar>
    A month view that starts on the user's first day of the week:

    <pre highlight=css>
    .month { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(7, 1fr); }
    .month .sun { order: 0; } .month .mon { order: 1; } /* … */ .month .sat { order: 6; }
    @media (first-day-of-week: monday) { .month .sun { order: 7; } }
    @media (first-day-of-week: saturday) { .month .sat { order: -1; } }
    </pre>
</div>

The date media queries [[DATE-MEDIA-QUERIES]] test the current date. These features describe how the user reads
dates, times, and measurements.


<h2 id=locale>The regional locale</h2>

The features in this specification are [=system media features=]. Their values come from a document's
<dfn export>regional locale</dfn>, which is a Unicode locale identifier:

1. If the document is in a [=reduced-exposure context=], or scripting is disabled for the document, the regional
    locale is the [=HTTP locale=].
1. Otherwise, it is the locale returned by [$DefaultLocale$]() in the document's realm: the locale that
    <code>Intl</code> uses when script doesn't give one.

The <dfn export>HTTP locale</dfn> is the first language tag in the <code>Accept-Language</code> header that the
user agent would send in a request from the document, with any Unicode locale extension removed. If the user agent
would send no such header, the HTTP locale is "und-001".

The <dfn>region</dfn> of a locale is the region subtag of the result of [=add likely subtags|adding likely
subtags=] to it [[!CLDR]]. If there is none, the region is "001", the world.

The features' values are found when the document is created, and don't change while it exists.

<h3 id=consistency>Consistency with script</h3>

A user agent may include a user's regional settings, such as a 24-hour clock chosen in the operating system, in
the regional locale, as [=Unicode locale extension keywords=] such as <code>hc</code>, <code>ms</code>,
<code>mu</code>, and <code>fw</code>. It may do so only if <code>Intl</code> uses the same settings in the same
document, so that script that formats a time without giving a locale formats it the way '@media/hour-cycle' says.

<div class=note>
    Some user agents apply operating system regional settings to <code>Intl</code>, and others deliberately don't,
    because the settings help identify users. This rule makes the features follow whatever the user agent already
    does for script.
</div>


<h2 id=features>Media features</h2>

<h3 id=hour-cycle>The '@media/hour-cycle' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: hour-cycle
Value: h12 | h23
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

Let |cycle| be the first hour cycle that <code>Intl.Locale.prototype.getHourCycles()</code> returns for the
[=regional locale=] [[INTL-LOCALE-INFO]]. That takes the <code>hc</code> keyword into account, and otherwise uses
[[!CLDR]] data for the locale's language and region.

: <dfn value for=@media/hour-cycle>h12</dfn>
:: |cycle| is "h11" or "h12": a 12-hour clock.
: <dfn value for=@media/hour-cycle>h23</dfn>
:: |cycle| is "h23" or "h24": a 24-hour clock.

<div class=note>
    "h11" (0 to 11, used in Japan) and "h24" (1 to 24) are rare, and are combined with the common clocks so they
    don't identify users.
</div>

<h3 id=measurement-system>The '@media/measurement-system' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: measurement-system
Value: metric | uksystem | ussystem
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

If the [=regional locale=] has the <code>ms</code> keyword, the feature is its value. Otherwise, it is the
measurement system that [[!CLDR]] gives for the locale's [=region=]:

: <dfn value for=@media/measurement-system>metric</dfn>
:: Metric units, such as kilometers and kilograms.
: <dfn value for=@media/measurement-system>uksystem</dfn>
:: A mix of metric and imperial units, such as miles and stones, as in the United Kingdom.
: <dfn value for=@media/measurement-system>ussystem</dfn>
:: US customary units, such as miles and pounds.

<div class=note>
    At the time of writing, CLDR gives ''ussystem'' for the United States and Liberia, ''uksystem'' for the United
    Kingdom and Myanmar, and ''metric'' for every other region.
</div>

<h3 id=temperature-unit>The '@media/temperature-unit' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: temperature-unit
Value: celsius | fahrenheit
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

If the [=regional locale=] has the <code>mu</code> keyword, the feature is ''fahrenheit'' if its value is
"fahrenhe", and ''celsius'' otherwise. If not, it is ''fahrenheit'' if [[!CLDR]] gives the US measurement system
for temperature in the locale's [=region=], and ''celsius'' otherwise.

: <dfn value for=@media/temperature-unit>celsius</dfn>
:: Degrees Celsius.
: <dfn value for=@media/temperature-unit>fahrenheit</dfn>
:: Degrees Fahrenheit.

<div class=note>
    At the time of writing, CLDR gives Fahrenheit for the United States, the Bahamas, Belize, the Cayman Islands,
    Palau, and Puerto Rico. Kelvin is combined with Celsius.
</div>

<h3 id=first-day-of-week>The '@media/first-day-of-week' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: first-day-of-week
Value: monday | tuesday | wednesday | thursday | friday | saturday | sunday
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The feature is the first day of the week for the [=regional locale=], as
<code>Intl.Locale.prototype.getWeekInfo()</code> reports it [[INTL-LOCALE-INFO]], with 1 as ''monday'' through
7 as ''sunday''. That takes the <code>fw</code> keyword into account, and otherwise uses [[!CLDR]] data for the
locale's region.


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What the features reveal</h3>

Without the user's own settings, every value follows from the language and region of the locale: a user whose
browser asks for "en-US" gets a 12-hour clock, US units, Fahrenheit, and Sunday. The locale is already sent to every
server in the <code>Accept-Language</code> header, and is available to script through
<code>navigator.language</code> and <code>Intl</code>.

With the user's own settings, the values can differ from the locale's defaults. A user with "en-US" who reads
a 24-hour clock, metric units, and weeks starting on Monday may have grown up somewhere else, or may work in
science, medicine, or the military. Settings like these can suggest where a user comes from, which can be used to
discriminate against them.

<h3 id=privacy-existing>What is already exposed</h3>

The design depends on two facts:

* **Script.** In a document with script, the values are required to match <code>Intl</code>
    ([[#consistency]]). Script can already format a time and see which clock it gets, and call
    <code>getWeekInfo()</code>. The features reveal nothing new to script, and if a user agent doesn't give the
    user's settings to <code>Intl</code>, the features don't have them either.
* **HTTP.** In documents without script, and in [=reduced-exposure contexts=], the values come only from the
    language tag that the user agent sends to servers in every request anyway. A style sheet that reports them
    reveals what the server already has.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Feature<th>Values<th>From the locale alone<th>With the user's settings
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>'@media/hour-cycle'<td>2<td>Nothing new<td>As much as <code>Intl</code> reveals
        <tr><td>'@media/measurement-system'<td>3<td>Nothing new<td>Not revealed by <code>Intl</code> today; a user
            agent reveals it only by adding <code>ms</code> to the locale <code>Intl</code> uses
        <tr><td>'@media/temperature-unit'<td>2<td>Nothing new<td>The same, with <code>mu</code>
        <tr><td>'@media/first-day-of-week'<td>7<td>Nothing new<td>As much as <code>getWeekInfo()</code> reveals
</table>

The values are also coarser than the settings: '@media/hour-cycle' combines four clocks into two, and
'@media/temperature-unit' combines Kelvin with Celsius.

<h3 id=privacy-correlation>Linking visits</h3>

The values don't change while a document exists, so there is no moment of change that could link documents.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

The values are the same in cross-origin frames as in the top-level document. A frame's script has the same
<code>Intl</code>, and its requests carry the same <code>Accept-Language</code> header.

<h3 id=privacy-locale-proposal>Relationship to User Locale Preferences</h3>

The User Locale Preferences proposal [[USER-LOCALE-PREFERENCES]] would give servers, when they ask, and script
the user's hour cycle, measurement system, first day of the week, and other settings. If it is adopted, a user agent
should include in the regional locale only settings that it would also give through that proposal, so that the
features reveal no more than it does.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* In a document with script, a user agent that gives the user's settings to <code>Intl</code> gives them to the
    features too. A style sheet can then report them without script in that document, which script there could
    already do.
* The locale itself, which these features follow, can suggest the user's nationality or where they live.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* The features describe how the user prefers to read things. They are not the user's location, citizenship, or
    legal jurisdiction, and pages must not use them to decide which laws apply, what a user is allowed to see, or
    what taxes to charge.
* The user controls the values, so pages must not rely on them for anything that matters for security.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-region>A region or locale feature</h3>

A feature such as <code>(region: us)</code> would let authors write their own rules. It was not chosen because it
reveals the region directly, which is more than a page needs to choose units, and because authors would use it as
a location, which it isn't. '':lang()'' already matches the language of content.

<h3 id=alt-formats>Formatting in CSS</h3>

CSS could format times and quantities itself. That is a much larger feature, and it would still need to know the
user's preferences, which is what this specification provides.

<h3 id=alt-live>Updating when the settings change</h3>

The values could change when the user changes a setting while a document is open. That would give documents a
shared moment of change. Users rarely change these settings, and a reload picks up the change.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should there be a <code>paper-size</code> feature, with <code>a4</code> and <code>letter</code>, so that print style
sheets can choose a page size? CLDR has the data.

<p class=issue>Which days are the weekend depends on the region. Should there be a feature for it, which would also
answer an open issue in [[DATE-MEDIA-QUERIES]]?

<p class=issue>Should '@media/hour-cycle' distinguish <code>h11</code> for Japanese users, who read "0:30 PM"?


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: The user's clock, units, temperature scale, and first day of the week, so pages can show information the way
    the user reads it.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. The values are coarser than the settings, and match what <code>Intl</code> or
    <code>Accept-Language</code> already exposes.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: The user's settings can suggest where they come from. See [[#privacy-exposed]].
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: They reveal the user's settings only where <code>Intl</code> already does, and never without script.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Possibly the user's origin or profession, when their settings differ from their locale's defaults.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Regional settings, only as far as <code>Intl</code> already does.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None. The values don't change while a document exists.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: It doesn't, because <code>Intl</code> and <code>Accept-Language</code> don't.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: Modes that protect against fingerprinting use the HTTP locale, without the user's settings.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: It keeps the values it had when it was created, as <code>Intl</code> does.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a new way to read a value, such as CSS, reaches contexts that the existing ways, such as script, don't.
